#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055764} :activity/id #uuid "87315fc7-165f-5b3e-a832-348934ea724d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048229 :topic/id #uuid "8a462f76-1e1a-5c54-833e-8d3d6162407f" :topic/math-academy-id 86} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055764 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055933 :tutorial/content "When simplifying numerical expressions, it is generally preferable to avoid having radicals in the denominator. In cases where we wish to remove a radical from a denominator, we must **rationalize the denominator**.\n\nIf the denominator of an expression consists of two terms and includes one or more radicals, we rationalize it by multiplying both the numerator and the denominator by the **conjugate** of the denominator.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{1+\\sqrt{2}}\n$$\n\nTo find the conjugate of the denominator $1+\\sqrt{2}$, we *flip the sign* between the two terms. So, the conjugate of the denominator is\n\n$$\n1\\,{\\color{blue}{-}}\\,\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we start by multiplying both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $1-\\sqrt{2}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{1 + \\sqrt{2}} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1}{1 + \\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{2}}{1 - \\sqrt{2}} &=  \\\\\n(1 \\cdot (1 - \\sqrt{2}))/((1 + \\sqrt{2})(1 - \\sqrt{2})) &=  \\\\\n(1 - \\sqrt{2})/((1 + \\sqrt{2})(1 - \\sqrt{2})) &= \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n(a+b)(a-b) = a^2 - b^2\n$$\n\nApplying the difference of squares formula with\n\n$$\na=1, b=\\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nto simplify the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 - \\sqrt{2})/((1 + \\sqrt{2})(1 - \\sqrt{2})) &=  \\\\\n(1 - \\sqrt{2})/(1^{2} - (\\sqrt{2})^{2}) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1 - \\sqrt{2}}{1 - 2} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{1 - \\sqrt{2}}{-1} &=  \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2b34f4d4-8931-5c24-8872-dfa9420a1a87" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13463 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "57ebf44f-ed04-5203-ae79-eb57faf1422c" :step/math-academy-id 478487 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055765}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055765 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054757 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054500 :question/id #uuid "04b471e2-edcd-5f01-8677-e93ddc547a0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-18927"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be393dbe-03cc-5259-ba28-8f865537c57a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049943}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055282}] :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing Two Terms"} :step/id #uuid "dced9096-a47b-5cd3-80ff-98a83b2d683d" :step/math-academy-id 478488 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055766}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055766 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054758 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054501 :question/id #uuid "0e811568-769f-5e18-9c6c-5594c18330b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-18931"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eae5693c-a37e-58f4-8793-0875ef8e0fb8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054960}] :knowledge-point/title "Rationalizing Denominators Containing Two Surds"} :step/id #uuid "a083012b-d787-56bf-b5e8-8344e62d247c" :step/math-academy-id 478491 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055767}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055767 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054759 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054502 :question/id #uuid "5a664cc0-30ca-5097-9505-0755963b0f1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-18932"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "203f17e9-3efb-5637-ac02-2c31e5a8a632" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050818}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055283} {:d